'''Digital sol''' ({{Langx|bl|sol dijital}}; also abbreviated as '''e-sol'''), or '''Digital Currency Electronic Payment''' ({{Langx|bl|pagamento electronico de moneda dijital}}), is a {{W|central bank digital currency}} (CBDC) issued by Balisca's {{W|central bank}}, the [[Bank of Balisca]] (BdB). It was the second digital currency to be issued by a major economy (after [[China|China's]] [[W:Digital renminbi|digital renminbi]]), and was launched fully in 2022. While functionally similar to the digital renminbi in its technological capability to bypass commercial banks, the digital sol is ideologically distinct. It is designed on a "federated ledger" model that prevents centralized state surveillance of private citizens while mandating absolute financial transparency for corporations, cooperatives, and government officials. The digital sol is {{W|legal tender}} and has equivalent value with other [[Baliscan sol|forms of sol]], such as bills and coins. The development of the digital sol coincides with the Baliscan government's embrace of {{W|e-government}}, {{W|digital utopianism}} and {{W|techno-progressivism}}.

== Purpose and ideology ==
Baliscan economic theory posits that money is a public utility rather than a commercial product, a belief that drove the development of the digital sol to address specific requirements of the confederation's [[W:Decentralized planning|decentralized economic model]]. Its primary function is disintermediation, allowing every citizen to hold a deposit account directly with the [[Bank of Balisca|Confederal Bank]] through local [[W:Credit union|credit unions]], effectively eliminating the need for a for-profit commercial banking industry that Baliscan law classifies as "[[W:Rent-seeking|rent-seeking]]." Furthermore, the currency is essential for ecological accounting; it supports "metadata tracking," meaning that when a cooperative purchases raw materials, the transaction carries data regarding the carbon footprint of those materials, allowing administrations to automate the collection of the luxury and waste tax (ILR) at the [[W:Point of sale|point of sale]]. Ideally, this system also facilitates frictionless redistribution, allowing enterprises to distribute surplus "shares" to worker wallets instantly without payroll processing fees.

== Key features ==
The digital sol is considered "programmable money," incorporating rules into its code that reflect Baliscan social values, most notably through a [[W:demurrage currency|demurrage mechanism]] inspired by the [[W:Freiwirtschaft|economic proposals]] of [[W:Silvio Gesell|Silvio Gesell]]. To prevent the re-emergence of a rentier class, savings accounts are subject to a "security cap" of 500,000 sol (approx. $375,000 equivalent); balances exceeding this cap that remain stagnant for over 12 months without being invested or donated are subject to a [[W:Negative interest on excess reserves|negative interest rate]], forcing capital to circulate rather than accumulate. Additionally, the currency simplifies fiscal policy through algorithmic taxation. Instead of filing annual tax returns, the system uses a split payment protocol where payments for non-essential luxury items are instantly divided, with the cost going to the seller and the tax routed automatically to the Treasury.

To enforce the [[Equitable Society Compact]] and its strict income ratios, the digital sol utilizes a system of asymmetric privacy. Individual citizens enjoy the "iron shield," where wallets are encrypted using [[W:Zero-knowledge proof|zero-knowledge proofs]], preventing the confederal government from viewing specific purchasing habits without a judicial warrant. 

Conversely, institutional entities operate under the "glass house" protocol; the wallets of cooperatives, [[Essential industries|state-owned enterprises]], and public officials are "glass wallets" (''carteras de cristal''), meaning their balances and flows are publicly viewable on the blockchain. This transparency allows labor unions and citizens to audit entities in real-time to ensure compliance with pay ratios and surplus hiding.

== Governance and co-existence ==
Unlike centralized digital currencies controlled by a single state bank, the digital sol runs on a [[W:blockchain#Consortium blockchain|consortium/federated blockchain]]. Control of the ledger is distributed among a network of "nodes" held jointly by the Confederal Bank, the regions/cantons/communes, [[Trade unions in Balisca|labor syndicates]], and [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federations]]. This distribution ensures that no single entity can freeze an account or alter the code unilaterally; a "kill switch" or asset freeze requires a consensus of the nodes, providing a robust check against potential government tyranny. Despite the ubiquity of the digital sol, the [[Constitution of Balisca]] protects the right to use physical cash. The [[Baliscan sol|physical sol]] remains in wide circulation, particularly in rural regions like [[Trás-as-Montanyas]] and for private transactions, serving as a "failsafe" against total digitalization.

The Bank has maintained a {{W|Freedom of information laws by country|complete transparency policy in line with Baliscan law}} regarding the digital sol, and in order to assuage concerns over {{W|privacy rights}}, built the currency utilizing a {{W|Blockchain#Centralized Blockchain|centralized blockchain}}, a {{W|distributed ledger technology}} (DLT) in order to maintain a system of "sensible anonymity". The use of a decentralized ledger replicated across the distributed network offers enhanced availability and minimize single points of failure, and the use of cryptographic hashes ensures the integrity of transaction records. In Balisca in 2021, {{W|Electronic funds transfer|non-cash transactions}} represented 4 out of 5 payment transactions, totaling a sum of $1,952.35 billion.

